\chapter{Extended ADM 3+1 Hamiltonian Formulation}
\label{chap:adm}

\section{Overview}
The STSE-H v2.0 engine evolves spacetime using an extended Arnowitt–Deser–Misner 
(ADM) 3+1 decomposition. This chapter presents the full Hamiltonian formulation, 
including the supermetric, conjugate momentum, exotic stress-energy tensor, and 
the Matos–Tonchev potential governing superluminal metric deformation.

The ADM formalism decomposes spacetime into spatial hypersurfaces $\Sigma_t$ 
parameterized by coordinate time $t$, enabling numerical evolution of the 
metric under warp-field conditions.

\section{3+1 Spacetime Decomposition}
The spacetime metric $g_{\mu\nu}$ is decomposed as:
\begin{equation}
ds^2 = -\alpha^2 dt^2 + h_{ij}(dx^i + \beta^i dt)(dx^j + \beta^j dt),
\end{equation}
where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\alpha$ is the lapse function,
    \item $\beta^i$ is the shift vector,
    \item $h_{ij}$ is the induced 3-metric on $\Sigma_t$.
\end{itemize}

The extrinsic curvature is defined as:
\begin{equation}
K_{ij} = \frac{1}{2\alpha}
\left(
\dot{h}_{ij} - D_i \beta_j - D_j \beta_i
\right),
\end{equation}
where $D_i$ is the covariant derivative compatible with $h_{ij}$.

\section{Hamiltonian and Momentum Constraints}
The ADM Hamiltonian constraint is:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H} =
R^{(3)} + K^2 - K_{ij}K^{ij} - 16\pi G\,T_{\text{eff}} = 0,
\end{equation}
and the momentum constraint is:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{M}^i = D_j(K^{ij} - h^{ij}K) - 8\pi G\,S^i = 0.
\end{equation}

In STSE-H v2.0, the effective stress-energy tensor $T_{\text{eff}}$ includes 
exotic matter contributions responsible for warp-field compression.

\section{Extended Hamiltonian with Exotic Matter}
The total Hamiltonian is:
\begin{equation}
H_{\text{total}} = 
\frac{1}{2}
\left(
G^{-1}_{ijkl}\,\pi^{ij}\pi^{kl}
-
\sqrt{h}\left(
R^{(3)} - 16\pi G\,T_{\text{eff}}
\right)
\right),
\end{equation}
where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $G^{-1}_{ijkl}$ is the DeWitt supermetric,
    \item $\pi^{ij}$ is the conjugate momentum,
    \item $h = \det(h_{ij})$,
    \item $T_{\text{eff}}$ includes the Matos–Tonchev exotic potential.
\end{itemize}

\section{The DeWitt Supermetric}
The supermetric is defined as:
\begin{equation}
G^{-1}_{ijkl} = 
\frac{1}{2}
\left(
h_{ik}h_{jl} + h_{il}h_{jk} - h_{ij}h_{kl}
\right).
\end{equation}

It governs the kinetic term of the Hamiltonian and determines how metric 
perturbations propagate through the hypersurface.

\section{Conjugate Momentum}
The conjugate momentum is:
\begin{equation}
\pi^{ij} = \sqrt{h}\left(K^{ij} - h^{ij}K\right).
\end{equation}

In STSE-H v2.0, $\pi^{ij}$ is evolved using a symplectic RK-8 integrator 
implemented in CUDA for high-resolution warp-field simulations.

\section{Exotic Stress-Energy Tensor}
The exotic matter contribution is modeled as:
\begin{equation}
T_{\text{eff}} = T_{\text{standard}} + T_{\text{exotic}},
\end{equation}
where $T_{\text{exotic}}$ is derived from the Matos–Tonchev potential:
\begin{equation}
V_{\text{MT}}(\phi) = -\Omega_{\text{exotic}}\exp(-\lambda\phi^2),
\end{equation}
with $\Omega_{\text{exotic}} < 0$ enabling localized metric contraction.

\section{Hamiltonian Violation Index}
STSE-H v2.0 monitors constraint drift using:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_{\text{violation}} =
\left|
\frac{\mathcal{H}}{\sqrt{h}}
\right|.
\end{equation}

During Warp~3375 stress testing, the engine maintained:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_{\text{violation}} < 6.42\times10^{-14},
\end{equation}
well below the stability threshold.

\section{Numerical Evolution}
The evolution equations are:
\begin{align}
\dot{h}_{ij} &= 
\frac{\delta H_{\text{total}}}{\delta \pi^{ij}}, \\
\dot{\pi}^{ij} &= 
-\frac{\delta H_{\text{total}}}{\delta h_{ij}}.
\end{align}

These are solved using:
\begin{itemize}
    \item CUDA-accelerated metric deformation kernels,
    \item unified memory grids up to $256^3$ nodes,
    \item symplectic RK-8 integration,
    \item encrypted telemetry logging.
\end{itemize}

